- \(
\Delta H_o^0 \text{ (CO}_2 \text{)} = -400.8 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
- \(
\Delta H_0 \text{(CO)} = -450.8 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
- \(
\Delta H_o \text{(O}_2 \text{)} = 0 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  

Substituting the given amount of 3 moles:  
\[
\Delta H = 3 \times (-400.8) - 3 \cdot (-450.8) + 1 \cdot 0 = -1202.8 + 1452.8 = 230 \text{ kJ}
\]  
Thus, \(\Delta H = 230 \text{ kJ}\).  

Next, we calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction:  
\[
\Delta S = \sum \sum_{S__0(text{CO}_2)} - \sum \sum{S_{\text{CO}} + \text{O}_2}
\]  
The standard free energies for the reactions are:  
- \(
\Delta S_o^\text{ ( CO}_2 \text{)} = 287.1 \text{ J/mol K}
\]  
- \(
\Delta S_o \text{(CO)} = 220.8 \text{ J/mol K}
\]  
- \(
\Delta S_o \text{(O}_2 \text{)} = 200.8 \text{ J/mol K}
\]  

Substituting the given amount of 3 moles:  
\[
\Delta S = 3 \times 287.1 - 3 \times 220.8 - 1 \times 0 = 880.3 - 662.8 = 217.5 \text{ J/mol K}
\]  
Thus, \(\Delta S = 217.5 \text{ J/mol K}\).  

The change in entropy for the reaction is \(\boxed{217.5 \text{ J/K}}\).
