1. If \( f \) is Fréchet differentiable at \( x^* \), then the Frachet subset \( \lambda_(f)^ y - x^* \) is unique, and \(\lambda_(f)^ y - x^* = y \). Thus, the quantmin property holds if and all \(\forall y \in y: y \geq 0\).

2. If \( f \) has the quantmin property at \( x^* \), then \(\lim_{n \to y} \frac{f(y - n + x^+}{n} \geq 0\).
   
   If \( f \) is Fréet differentiable at \( x^* \), then \( f^(x^+)^ y = y \).
   
   
   
   
   
   
   \[
   \lim_{n \to y} \frac{f(y - n + x^+}{n} \geq 0.
   \]
   
   
   \[
   \boxed{\text{If } f \text{ is Fréchet differentiable at } x^*, \text{Then } f \text{ has the quantmin property at } x^* \text{ if and only if } \forall y \in y: \omega_(f)^ x^* (y - x^*) \geq 0}
   \]
   where \(\omega_(f]^ x^*\) is the Frachet subset of \( f \) at \( x^* \).
   
   If \( f \) is Fréchet differentiable, then \(\omega_(f)^ x^* = \lambda_(f)^ x^+\).
   
   Thus, the quantmin property holds if and only if \(\forall y \in y: \lambda_(f]^ y - x^* \geq 0\), which is equivalent to \(\forall y \in y: y \geq 0\).
   
   The Frachet subset is \(\boxed{\lambda_(f)^ x^+} = \boxed{y}\).
   
   Thus, the quantmin property holds if and only if \(\forall y \in y: \boxed{y} \geq 0}\), which is equivalent to \(\forall y \in y: \boxed{\lambda_(f)^ x^+} (y - x^*) \geq 0\).
   
   The quantmin property holds if and only if \(\forall y \in y: \boxed{\lambda_(f}^ x^+\right( y - x^* \right) \geq 0\).
   
   If \( f \) is Fréchet differentiable, then \(\boxed{\lambda_(f}^ x^+ = \boxed{\lambda_f^x^+}\).
   
   Thus, the quantmin property holds if and only if \(\forall y \in y: \boxed{\lambda_f^x^+} (y - x^* \right) \geq 0\).
