\]  
The standard free energy change (\(\Delta G^f^^0\)) for this reaction is \(\boxed{-30.5 \, \text{kJ/mol}}\).  

However, the actual free energy change (\(\Delta G^f\)) is not constant and depends on the concentrations of ATP, ADP, and Pi.  
The Gibbs equation is used to calculate the free energy change under non-standard conditions:  
\[
\Delta G^f = \Delta G^f^+ + RT \ln \left( \frac{[A][B]}{[B]^1} \right.
\]  
