</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The entropy production rate \( \Pi_S \) is given by the dissipated mechanical power divided by the temperature. The mechanical power dissipated by friction is:  
\[
P_W = F_{\text{fr}} \cdot v
\]  
Thus, the entropy production rate is:  
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{P_W}{T} = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} \cdot v}{T}
\]  
This represents the rate at which entropy is generated due to the irreversible frictional process.  

**Numerical Application (if needed):**  
For \( F_{\text{fr}} = 1 \, \text{N} \), \( v = 0.1 \, \text{m/s} \), and \( T = 25^\circ \text{C} = 298 \, \text{K} \), the entropy production rate is:  
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{1 \cdot 0.1}{298} \approx 3.36 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{W/K}
\]  

\boxed{\Pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} \cdot v}{T}}
</GROUND_TRUTH>

